Ah — yes. Schrödinger’s equation is especially interesting immediately after what we were discussing.

The time-dependent equation is:

i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi(\mathbf r,t)
=
\hat H\Psi(\mathbf r,t)

In plain English: the wavefunction \Psi represents the quantum state, and the Hamiltonian \hat H determines how that state evolves through time.

For one nonrelativistic particle in a potential V:

i\hbar\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial t}
=
\left[
-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\nabla^2+V
\right]\Psi

The connection to your screen thought is neat, as an analogy rather than a claim that UI behavior is quantum mechanics.

You just proposed treating the visible screen as an aperture onto a larger state-space. Schrödinger gives us another conceptual separation:

\boxed{\text{State} \neq \text{currently observed presentation of state}}

The wavefunction isn’t simply “the thing you see.” It encodes amplitudes over possible measurement outcomes. Measurement gives you a particular result according to the theory’s probability rules.

So abstract that computationally:

\text{Underlying State}
\xrightarrow{\text{evolution operator}}
\text{New State}
\xrightarrow{\text{observation/rendering}}
\text{Visible Representation}

That could improve the Semantic Display Field architecture. Instead of storing the interface itself as truth, maintain a richer latent semantic state S(t), evolve it according to operators, and let the viewport/rendering system expose only a projection:

S(t+\Delta t)=U(\Delta t)S(t)

Display = P_{\text{viewport,task}}(S)

Now moving sideways, zooming, switching layers, changing representation, etc. doesn’t necessarily change the underlying information. It changes the projection P through which you’re observing it.

That’s a powerful distinction for Pantheon:

State evolution changes what’s known/represented underneath.

Viewport transformation changes where you’re looking.

Representation transformation changes how it is rendered.

Observation/interaction determines what portion becomes foreground/actionable.

So Schrödinger’s equation gives us a useful architectural inspiration: don’t confuse the evolving state-space with any particular observation of it.

And that slots almost suspiciously well into the thing we were just building.